  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish that elevated-temperature deformation can reduce dislocation density, alter elastic or strength behavior, and trigger changes in deformation mechanisms in HEAs. R9 is especially close mechanistically, linking increasing deformation temperature to reduced dislocation density and a mechanism shift in CoNiCrFeMn nanowires; R6, R8, R11, and R18 provide related temperature-dependent dislocation and mechanical-property evidence. However, none describes temperature-controlled nanoindentation or friction simulation of an FeNiCrCoCu coating on Cu, nor the specific transition near 900 K from stacking-fault-mediated deformation to lattice disorder with rising friction. Thus, the literature points in the right mechanistic direction, but selecting the specific coated architecture, tribological intervention, temperature series, and coupled hardness-friction analysis would require a substantial new idea."
